  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\n...UPDATED AVIATION DISCUSSION...\n\nMarine layer low clouds have spread into parts of the valleys this\nmorning with otherwise mostly clear skies. Low clouds will\ncontinue to move into the inland valleys through sunrise, then\nretreat to the coast by late morning. Mid-level flow turns\nslightly more southerly today, bringing a marginal increase in\nmonsoonal moisture. There is a 15-20% chance of showers and a\n10-15% chance of thunderstorms over the mountains and high desert\nthis afternoon. Given how high based (700-500 mb) the moisture is,\ngusty outflow winds will be the primary threat. Otherwise, upper\nlevel troughing off the West Coast will maintain relatively cooler\nweather with high temperatures around 3 to 7 degrees below mid-\nAugust normals. Highs will be in the mid to upper 70s along the\ncoast, low to mid 80s for inland Orange County and the western\nvalleys, upper 80s to mid 90s for the inland valleys, mid 70s to\nupper 80s in the mountains, 90s in the high desert, and low 100s\nin the low deserts. High pressure begins to expand in from the\neast on Sunday for minor warming and more stable conditions,\nlimiting the potential for any afternoon showers and thunderstorms\nover the mountains. This will also lead to a more shallow marine\nlayer with low clouds not spreading as far inland Sunday morning.\n\nThe upper level ridge of high pressure continues to expand\nwestward and strengthen through at least Thursday, bringing\ngradual warming and a shallower marine layer. High temperatures\nreturn to near normal by Monday, then rise to around 3 to 7\ndegrees above normal on Thursday. Though some mid-level moisture\nlingers in mostly southerly flow, the ridge axis will be overhead\nand the subsidence inversion will act as a lid on any potential\nconvection. While there may be some shallow cumulus over the\nmountains in the afternoon, shower/thunderstorm chances are less\nthan 10%. While air temperatures themselves are nothing notable\nfor this time of year, muggy conditions and warm nights will push\nthe HeatRisk into moderate for parts of the valleys, lower\nelevation mountains, and deserts Wednesday through Saturday, with\nlocally high HeatRisk in the low deserts. \n\nEnsembles begin to diverge on the position of the upper level high\nand a trough off the coast late in the week. Around 46% of the\nmembers have the trough further east (closer to the coast), which\nwould provide some cooling, while the other 54% have the trough \nfurther off the coast with warmer weather here. The position of\nthe trough and to a greater extent the upper high over the desert\nsouthwest will determine how much monsoon moisture hangs around.\nFor now model consensus generally has the upper high in a more\nfavorable position for convection Friday into next weekend.",
